Suggested order from the review: T2 + T5 first (CI + corpus test, makes everything
after it verifiable), then L1, then T1 + L2-L4, then M1.

NOTE (kept for reference): "assert zero errors on files that compile standalone"
is weaker than it sounds - 64tass skips unreferenced .proc bodies, so a file that is
one big .proc passes trivially while validating nothing. The T5 corpus fixtures
reference every proc so their bodies are genuinely assembled.

optional: case-insensitive labels

-----------------------

SERVER LIFECYCLE (all in server.ts, which has no tests)

L1 (High) - configuration changes never reach the server  [DONE]
	connection.onDidChangeConfiguration cannot fire: vscode-languageclient only
	sends workspace/didChangeConfiguration if the client sets
	synchronize.configurationSection or the server dynamically registers for it,
	and we do neither (clientOptions.synchronize is {} in extension.ts).
	So changing 64tass.caseSensitive does nothing until the window is reloaded -
	contradicting the CHANGELOG's "Index automatically rebuilds when setting changes".
	fixed: registers DidChangeConfigurationNotification.type in onInitialized (gated
	on the client's didChangeConfiguration.dynamicRegistration capability, which is
	NOT the same as workspace.configuration that hasConfigurationCapability tracks).
	The handler now also clears include refs and re-publishes diagnostics for all
	open documents. This also makes D3 true - see below.

L2 (Medium) - closing an included file drops it from the index  [DONE]
	onDidClose calls documentIndex.delete(uri) unconditionally. If that file is
	.include'd by a still-open document, the parent loses every symbol it provided.
	includeRefCount already holds what's needed to decide this.
	fixed: the ref-counting moved out of server.ts into IncludeGraph (includes.ts)
	so it is unit-testable; onDidClose now keeps the index entry when another root
	still includes the file. Partially addresses T1 as well.

L3 (Medium) - re-indexing reads includes from disk, discarding unsaved buffers  [DONE]
	indexDocument always fs.readFileSync's an included file even when it is open with
	unsaved edits, so editing the parent silently reverts the include's index.
	fixed: indexDocument now uses the open buffer for an include that is open, and
	getDocumentText otherwise; onReferences' duplicate copy of that logic was folded
	into getDocumentText too. NOT unit-tested - indexDocument still lives in
	server.ts and would need its dependencies injected to be testable (see T1).

L4 (Medium) - diagnostics published only for the document that changed  [DONE]
	Editing an include never refreshes the parent's diagnostics. No
	didChangeWatchedFiles registration either, so edits made outside the editor are
	invisible.
	fixed: publishDiagnosticsFor(uri) refreshes every OPEN root whose include tree the
	document participates in (via IncludeGraph.affectedRoots, which is unit-tested);
	used by both the edit path and the config-change path. Also registers a
	didChangeWatchedFiles watcher for **/*.{asm,s,inc,src} so external edits re-index
	- open documents are skipped there, since their buffer is authoritative.

L5 (Low) - no workspace scan on startup  [DONE]
	Only open documents and their include trees are indexed, so go-to-definition and
	find-references silently return nothing for symbols in unopened files, with no
	signal distinguishing "not found" from "not looked at".
	This is the main remaining source of "Undefined symbol" noise in the corpus.
	fixed: scanWorkspace() runs in the BACKGROUND after startup (not awaited, yields
	to the event loop every 20 files) so a big tree does not delay initialize -
	the example corpus is 91 files / 83k lines and takes ~780ms to parse.
	The directory walk is collectSourceFiles() in workspace.ts, unit-tested:
	skips node_modules/.git/out/dist/build, does not follow symlinked dirs, capped
	at WORKSPACE_SCAN_LIMIT (5000) with a warning when it truncates.
	CAVEAT: scanned files are indexed standalone, so a file that is part of a
	compilation unit gets the global caseSensitive setting rather than its root's
	cascaded value until that root is actually opened.
	NOTE: this should shrink the "Undefined symbol" noise a lot in a real workspace,
	and is what unlocks M2 (workspaceSymbol) and most of the remaining conditional
	warnings noted in the design call above.

-----------------------

DESIGN CALL - symbols in inactive .if branches: DECIDED & DONE
	Chose option B (evaluate constant conditions, suppress only provably-dead
	branches) plus a "; 64tass-langserv: define NAME = VALUE" pragma emulating
	64tass's -D flag, so build-time flags can be resolved too.
	Corpus effect: undefined-symbol warnings 559 -> 538.
	Note ~36 of the remaining conditional warnings are undecidable only because the
	flag lives in a file we never indexed - fixing L5 (or adding a define pragma)
	is what unlocks those, not more evaluator work.

-----------------------

MISSING CAPABILITIES (ordered by value/effort; first three are nearly free)

M1 - [DONE] documentSymbol: outline, breadcrumbs and Ctrl+Shift+O.
     buildDocumentSymbols() in documentSymbols.ts (unit-tested; server.ts is thin glue).
     Nests by directive scope, and local _name symbols under their code label.
     Anonymous labels are omitted - they have no name and would be pure noise.
     Scope ranges are derived from scopeAtLine so a scope spans its BODY, not just
     its name; without that, breadcrumbs only light up when the cursor is on the
     name itself. Note Range.create rejects MAX_SAFE_INTEGER (LSP caps a character
     at uinteger max), hence the END_OF_LINE constant.
M2 - [DONE] workspaceSymbol (Ctrl+T), unblocked by L5. findWorkspaceSymbols() in
     workspaceSymbols.ts, unit-tested. Subsequence matching ("sinit" finds
     "sprite_init"), ranked exact > prefix > substring > subsequence, capped at 500.
     Anonymous labels omitted; a local symbol reports its code label as container.
M3 - prepareRename. DONE as part of the C8 fix (bfa4fd8).
M4 - [DONE] signatureHelp for macro and function calls, from parametersAtScope.
     signatureHelp.ts, unit-tested. Handles all three call forms the assembler
     accepts (verified): "#mac a, b", ".mac a, b", "fn(a, b)" - including a call
     after a label, nested parens, and commas inside nested calls.
M5 - definition on .binclude / .binary paths (onDefinition handles .include only,
     while completions.ts already covers all three).
M6 - documentHighlight (occurrences of the symbol under the cursor).
M7 - semantic tokens, to disambiguate what the TextMate grammar cannot.
M8 - no debounce on validation: every keystroke re-indexes the whole include tree
     and runs a full validation pass.

-----------------------

TEST SUITE

T1  - server.ts has (almost) no tests, and 5 of the 13 defects lived there.
      PARTLY ADDRESSED: include ref-counting extracted to includes.ts + tested.
      Still to extract: computeFoldingRanges, so folding is testable at all
      (the C6 fix touched the folding path and could NOT be covered by a test).
T2  - [DONE] .github/workflows/ci.yml runs on push to main, PRs and manual dispatch:
      yarn install --frozen-lockfile, typecheck, test, compile. All four verified
      locally as the exact sequence CI runs.
      NOTE: the integration tests (compiler-ref) will SKIP in CI since 64tass is not
      installed there - see T4, which makes that visible rather than silent.
T3  - [DONE] release.yml now uses yarn install --frozen-lockfile and yarn
      compile:prod (was npm install, which ignored yarn.lock). Also gated the
      release on yarn typecheck + yarn test, closing T2 for the release path too.
      vsce's --no-yarn flags were left alone: node_modules/** is vsceignored and
      esbuild bundles everything, so the package manager cannot affect the .vsix.
T4  - [DONE] TASS_PATH is now env-overridable, a missing binary prints a visible
      [integration] warning instead of skipping silently, and REQUIRE_TASS=1 makes
      it a hard failure. All three modes verified.
      NOTE: CI does NOT set REQUIRE_TASS, so the compiler-reference suite still
      skips there (20 tests). Installing 64tass in CI + setting REQUIRE_TASS=1 would
      close that properly - left open deliberately, since it needs a build of the
      assembler in the workflow.
T5  - [DONE] test/integration/corpus.test.ts over test/fixtures/corpus/*.asm.
      Deliberately uses TRACKED fixtures, not example/, which is gitignored and so
      would not exist in CI.
      Each fixture is verified to assemble (with 64tass, skipped if absent) BEFORE
      the zero-error assertions - otherwise a fixture could quietly stop compiling
      and the assertions would prove nothing. Fixtures avoid the unreferenced-.proc
      trap described in the NOTE at the top: every proc is referenced.
      Currently 0 errors and 0 warnings; the warning budget is asserted at 0.
      Verified it has teeth: reverting the C4 float fix and the dead-branch
      suppression makes it fail.
T6  - [DONE] test/integration/case-sensitivity.test.ts assembles the same source
      with and without -C and checks the validator agrees in the matching mode -
      so the "equivalent to -C" claim is tested against the assembler, not just
      against our own model. Covers: differently-cased reference resolving without
      -C but not with it, exact-case resolving under -C, and two symbols differing
      only by case being distinct under -C / a duplicate without it.
T7  - [DONE] buildIndex() now takes caseSensitive PER ENTRY, falling back to the
      first entry's value so every existing call site behaves exactly as before.
      Added tests building one case-sensitive and one case-insensitive document in
      a single index and checking each resolves under its own rules - the case the
      per-document pragma cascade exists to handle.
T8  - [DONE] added rename tests for: renaming across files (definition in one, uses
      in another, plus an unreadable document being skipped), the comment-edit path
      (documentChanges + needsConfirmation annotation, and that ONLY the comment
      occurrence is annotated), and invalid new names.
      NOTE: "rejection of invalid identifiers" was not just an untested path - there
      was no rejection at all. Renaming to "1abc" or "foo bar" would have written
      un-assemblable text at every reference, the same class of bug as C8. Added
      isValidSymbolName() (pattern verified against the assembler), refused in
      computeRenameEdits so no caller can bypass it, with a clear message from the
      rename handler.
T9  - [DONE] added range assertions: a generic check that every diagnostic's range
      covers exactly the name its message quotes, plus specific column checks and a
      well-formedness check.
      operandStart no longer uses indexOf - it is derived from the match length,
      since both operand patterns are anchored and end with (.+)$. (The indexOf
      risk turned out to be nearly unreachable in practice: the operand is the whole
      operand string, and a single-token operand matching an earlier label would be
      defined, so there would be no diagnostic to misplace. Fixed anyway.)
      The range assertions DID find a real inconsistency: "Undefined macro" covered
      ".name" while quoting "name". Now points at the name, matching where 64tass
      itself points (verified).
T10 - performance.test.ts is a benchmark in the unit directory, running and appending
      to performance-results.jsonl on every yarn test.
      WON'T DO - explicitly left as-is by request.
T11 - [DONE] @vitest/coverage-v8 installed, `yarn test:coverage` added, config in
      vitest.config.ts (src only; extension.ts and performance.ts excluded as pure
      wiring / benchmark instrumentation). coverage/ is gitignored.
      Baseline: 75.7% lines overall - and that number is almost entirely server.ts,
      which is 0% of 267 lines. Every other module is 94-100%. That is T1 in one
      number: the untested code is exactly the code that is hard to test.
T12 - [DONE] eslint 9 + typescript-eslint installed with a flat config
      (eslint.config.mjs); the script is now `eslint .` (v9 dropped --ext) and CI
      runs it. Passes with 0 errors.
      It immediately found 4 real problems, all fixed: three unnecessary \- escapes
      in anonymous-label character classes (verified the patterns behave identically
      without them before changing) and a dead createDoc import in security.test.ts.
      The 9 remaining warnings are deliberate `any` at the LSP/JSON boundary.

